    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence.jpg" alt="Boso of Provence" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence">Boso of Provence</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Boson_of_Provence.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, often called the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, was formed by the union of two kingdoms that had emerged from the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. In 879 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence">Boso of Vienne</a> was elected king at Mantaille, founding the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a>, or Provence, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-blind">Louis the Blind</a>, crowned emperor in 901, was blinded at Verona in 905, and real power passed to his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-arles">Hugh of Arles</a>, who became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 926. In 888 the Welf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-burgundy">Rudolf I</a> had been crowned at the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-maurice-dagaune">Saint-Maurice</a> d’Agaune as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a>, the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennine-alps">Pennine Alps</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Rudolf II</a> also ruled Italy for a few years, until Hugh displaced him. Around 933, according to the usual reading of the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liutprand-of-cremona">Liutprand of Cremona</a>, the two men made a bargain: Rudolf renounced his claims in Italy and Hugh ceded his rights in Lower Burgundy. The date and terms of the agreement are uncertain, and Rudolf’s control of Provence was probably limited. When Rudolf died in 937, Hugh tried to take over the inheritance by marrying his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertha-of-kent">Bertha</a>, but Otto I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> took Rudolf’s young son Conrad under his protection and secured the throne for him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lyon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyon.jpg" alt="Lyon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyon-part-dieu-2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aldg692VX, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of Provence, embracing very different landscapes: Alpine valleys and pastures, the vineyards and grain fields of the Rhône corridor, and the olive groves, salt marshes and ports of the south. Its wealth came largely from its position on routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-st-bernard-pass">Great St Bernard Pass</a> and other Alpine crossings linked it with Italy, the Rhône carried traffic between the Mediterranean and the north, and cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and, above all, Marseille grew as commercial centres from the eleventh century. Society was dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, abbots and noble families. Great monasteries included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-maurice-dagaune">Saint-Maurice</a> d’Agaune, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-victor-marseille">Saint-Victor of Marseille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lerins-abbey">Lérins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmajour-abbey">Montmajour</a> near Arles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluniac-reforms">Cluniac</a> priory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/payerne">Payerne</a>, associated with Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertha-of-kent">Bertha</a>. From the late ninth century Muslim raiders based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a>, near modern La Garde-Freinet on the Provençal coast, plundered the region and the Alpine passes, until their capture of Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majolus-of-cluny">Majolus of Cluny</a> in 972 provoked Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-provence">William of Provence</a> to destroy their base. Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> were also centres of the Peace and Truce of God movement, and from 1309 the popes resided at Avignon, which they bought outright in 1348.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Rudolf III of Burgundy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">Rudolf III of Burgundy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donation_du_roi_de_Bourgogne_Rodolphe_III_%C3%A0_l%27abbaye_Saint-Maurice_(15_f%C3%A9vrier_1018)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom never developed a strong royal army. Its kings relied on the service of counts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and real military power lay with regional lords. The most important military achievement of the tenth century was not royal but comital: around 973 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-provence">William of Provence</a>, later called the Liberator, and his allies defeated the Muslim garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a>, after which the counts of Provence distributed the recovered lands among their followers. Defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, who raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in the 920s and 930s, also fell largely to local lords and bishops. The succession of 1032 was settled by war. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-ii-of-blois">Odo II of Blois</a>, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">Rudolf III</a>, claimed the kingdom and invaded, but Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Conrad II</a> campaigned in Burgundy in 1033 and 1034, forced Odo to withdraw and received the submission of the Burgundian lords. Thereafter the kingdom’s wars were mostly local struggles among its great lords, such as the long conflict between the counts of Savoy and the dauphins of Viennois in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries, or imperial expeditions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>’s, which relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">Zähringen</a> rectors and on alliances with local nobles rather than on any kingdom-wide levy. Castles, fortified towns and episcopal cities dominated the countryside. By the fourteenth century French royal armies and officials, not imperial ones, were the strongest force along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-burgundy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Conrad I of Burgundy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-burgundy">Conrad I of Burgundy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denier_of_Conrad_the_Peaceful.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CGB, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal government in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> was weak from the start. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-burgundy">Conrad the Peaceful</a>, who reigned from 937 to 993, owed his throne to Otto I, married Matilda, a daughter of the Carolingian King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-of-france">Louis IV of France</a>, and kept the peace largely through cooperation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and great lords. His sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-italy">Adelaide</a> married Otto I in 951 and became empress, tying the kingdom closely to the Ottonian court. The kings resided mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, and had little direct authority in Provence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">Rudolf III</a>, who ruled from 993 to 1032, tried to strengthen his position by granting counties to loyal bishops, giving the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> to the bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sion-switzerland">Sion</a> in 999 and the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> to the bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> in 1011, and in agreements from 1006 onwards he recognised his nephew, Emperor Henry II, as his heir. After 1032 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> became the third kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The emperors ruled it through occasional visits, charters and local allies, and from 1127 the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">Zähringen</a> held the office of rector of Burgundy, founding towns such as Fribourg and Bern. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>’s marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-i-countess-of-burgundy">Beatrice of Burgundy</a> in 1156 and his coronation at Arles in 1178 renewed imperial claims, and the archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> bore the title of arch-chancellor for the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arles.jpg" alt="Arles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_arles_france_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chensiyuan, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> left a fainter mark on later history than most medieval kingdoms, but its existence shaped the map of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Because it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> remained for centuries outside the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and their gradual absorption by France, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1312 and the Dauphiné in 1349 to Provence in 1481, was one of the long processes of French state-building. The weakness of royal power allowed new political units to grow within it: the County of Provence, the Dauphiné of Viennois, the Free County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, the County of Savoy, which became a major Alpine state and later gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> its royal dynasty, and the cities and bishoprics of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, whose lands later joined the Swiss Confederation. The marriage of Conrad’s sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-italy">Adelaide</a> to Otto I linked Burgundian lands to the Ottonian imperial project, and the kingdom’s incorporation in 1032 made the emperors rulers of a realm whose title, King of Arles, survived long after any real authority had gone. The region was also a cradle of the Peace and Truce of God, proclaimed in Provence and Burgundy in the early eleventh century, and Provence later became, through its troubadours and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, a major centre of medieval culture.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Charles I of Anjou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Some_Old_Guy_2_(5642308121)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Son of Groucho from Scotland, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imperial authority in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> faded steadily from the late twelfth century, as the emperors were absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> grew stronger along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, acquired the County of Provence by marrying its heiress Beatrice in 1246, bringing it into the orbit of the French royal family and later of the Angevin kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, formally an imperial city, in 1312, and the popes had settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> from 1309. In 1349 the last independent dauphin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-ii-of-viennois">Humbert II</a>, transferred the Dauphiné of Viennois to the heir of the French throne, and from then on the eldest son of the French king bore the title of dauphin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> tried to revive imperial claims and was crowned king at Arles in 1365, the last such coronation. But during his visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1378 he appointed the French dauphin, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI</a>, imperial vicar of the kingdom for life, effectively surrendering imperial authority in the Rhône valley. The kingdom ceased to function as a political entity. Its remaining lands passed gradually to France, to Savoy and to the Swiss Confederation, and only the title survived among the emperors’ dignities.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> united Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a> in the tenth century and in 1032 became the third crown of the Holy Roman emperors, but its weak monarchy let counts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and dynasties such as Savoy and the dauphins of Viennois take real power, and its lands were gradually absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Savoy and the Swiss.</em></p>

